WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.170
So my name is Logan,
Logan Engstrom.

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.312
And I'm a computer
science major at MIT.

00:00:05.312 --> 00:00:07.520
And I'm going to talk about
the trade-offs associated

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.600
with robust machine learning.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.430
So in machine learning
recently, there's

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.500
been an explosion in the
use of neural networks

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.940
to do image tasks.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.950
And neural networks
have absolutely

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:21.920
blown away the competition
on a bunch of standard image

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.080
classification tasks.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.450
But they all suffer from
the same vulnerability

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.070
of adversarial examples.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.740
Adversarial examples
are examples

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.157
where you take an input and then
you perturb it very slightly.

00:00:34.157 --> 00:00:35.990
And to a human, you
wouldn't be able to tell

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:37.180
the difference between
the original input

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.450
and the perturbed input.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.970
But to the classifier,
you totally fool it.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.810
So for example, you can
take a picture of this pig.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.600
You can add a little
bit of perturbation that

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.850
looks like noise to a human.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.433
And that you can't even tell the
difference between this image

00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:52.490
and that image actually.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.980
And it turns out that this
image with the added noise

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.390
classifies as an airplane
instead of a pig.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.650
So this is a really big problem.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.850
It's prevalent throughout
all state of the art image

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:02.870
classification models.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.370
And there's been a
huge line of work

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.180
on trying to fix this problem.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.960
And it turns out that the
most successful methods

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.660
for fixing this problem
for making networks that

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.200
are more robust also
caused classifiers

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.800
to have a significantly lower
accuracy than just training

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.310
naturally, and making a
model that has high accuracy

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.970
but is not very robust.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.660
So what we set out to
do is try to figure out

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.880
if there's really a
fundamental trade-off here,

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.590
or if we're just not
doing things correctly.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.620
So we start out with
a theoretical model.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.360
In our theoretical
model, we essentially

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.930
show that there does
exist a setting where

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.220
there is a fundamental
trade-off between accuracy

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:40.900
and robustness.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.530
And it's a pretty natural model.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.930
And this statement is
essentially of the form

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.300
that if you reach a
certain amount of accuracy,

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.880
you have to have at most this
amount of robust accuracy,

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.020
and vise versa.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.760
Now, we have this hypothesis.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.040
We now move on to actually
verifying it in the real world.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.870
Using classifiers
that are actually

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.180
used in the real world
on real world data sets.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:03.830
We complete a bunch
of experiments

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.200
to verify our hypothesis.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.350
We showed that in the
large sample regime,

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:12.890
if you tried to make classifiers
that are significantly more

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.280
robust than the naturally
trained classifiers,

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.300
then there is a
fundamental trade-off here.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.330
At least in the classifiers
that we have today.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:21.830
But in the small
sample regime, you

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.560
can actually see that making
classifiers more robust

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.855
actually helps.

00:02:25.855 --> 00:02:27.230
And it essentially
acts as a form

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:30.590
of data augmentation to make
your classifiers even more

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:31.230
accurate.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.730
But in a large
sample regime, we see

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.292
the same predicted
phenomena that we

00:02:35.292 --> 00:02:36.500
saw in our theoretical model.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.375
And the large sample
regime is what we really

00:02:38.375 --> 00:02:40.770
care about in the real world.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.610
Now, we have these
problems with training

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:44.780
these robust classifiers.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.760
But are they really problems?

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.830
And is there a kind of
a silver lining here?

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.885
And so it turns out that these
robust classifiers are actually

00:02:51.885 --> 00:02:53.510
more aligned with
human vision in terms

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.635
of how they classify images.

00:02:55.635 --> 00:02:56.510
And this makes sense.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.300
Because you can essentially
think of robust classifiers

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.820
as being more invariant around--

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:04.850
in a very small L2-norm ball--

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.650
around every single example.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.750
And in human terms,
that's basically

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.695
like you have some
kind of image.

00:03:10.695 --> 00:03:12.820
And then if you had a very
small perturbation to it

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.500
that a human couldn't tell
the difference between,

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.060
then a human's not going to be
able to tell the difference,

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:18.200
tautologically.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.020
And so if we make it so
that these classifiers are

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.843
more robust just
around the example,

00:03:22.843 --> 00:03:24.260
then that means
that they're going

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.260
to be more human-- they're going
to be more aligned with the way

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.680
that human vision works.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.440
And we can actually
see this when

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.990
trying to interpret the way
that these classifiers make

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:33.950
decisions.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.710
So you can see here, this
is a beetle, or an insect.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.530
And we're trying to classify it
as an insect or a dog or a cat,

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:41.090
or one of any other labels.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.730
And so with a
standard classifier

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.260
that's been trained
without trying

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:49.355
to make the classifier
robust, the heat map

00:03:49.355 --> 00:03:51.230
of what the classifier
occurs about basically

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:52.610
looks like noise.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.450
And so you can kind
of see that there's

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.130
like a vague
outline of a beetle.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:03:59.930
But if you were to
see this a priori,

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.270
you'd have no idea why
this classifier was

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.200
making this decision.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.580
But it turns out that when you
add this robustness guarantee,

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.320
the gradients become
much more aligned.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:10.970
That is, the things--

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.753
the parts of the image that
the classifier cares about

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:15.170
becomes significantly
more aligned

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:16.862
with what humans
actually care about.

00:04:16.862 --> 00:04:19.070
And so you can see here in
this gradient in this heat

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:21.740
map of what the
classifier cares about,

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.698
you can see that
there's essentially

00:04:23.698 --> 00:04:24.740
an outline of the beetle.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.650
And you can see why this
classifier made this decision.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:31.040
This is the original example
that we're trying to classify.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.790
This is the heat map of what
the standard classifier cares

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:36.500
about.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.160
This right here is
the heat map of what

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.350
the adversarily-trained
robust classifier cares about.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.260
And you can see it's
much more aligned.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.630
We have these
experiments about showing

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:51.835
what happens when you have
large norm adversarial examples.

00:04:51.835 --> 00:04:53.210
So this is basically
saying, what

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:54.980
happens if the
classifier-- what happens

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:00.040
if we can make
adversarial examples that

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.760
can be as far away as we
want from the classifier.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.110
And so from the original input.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:08.770
So instead of making it so that
the adversarial examples have

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:10.603
to be imperceptible
from the original image,

00:05:10.603 --> 00:05:13.020
we're going to make it so they
can be as big as they want.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.010
It turns out that when
you make a large norm

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:17.470
adversarial example starting
from this guy with a SARA

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.290
classifier, you basically
get the same monkey.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:23.440
But when you try to make
the same adversarial example

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.528
from this guy going to the L2
trained adversarial example,

00:05:26.528 --> 00:05:28.570
you actually get something
that looks like a dog.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.130
Showing that these classifiers,
to really fool them,

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.860
you actually have
to make images that

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.470
look like the adversarial
class that you're

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:37.560
trying to fool them with.

